{
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "The excess distance was caused by a genuine road incident, not by the driver cutting a corner: the driver told the rider at 10:24:30 that there was a breakdown inside the KPE tunnel, and the LTA traffic record for that same KPE segment confirms a VEHICLE_BREAKDOWN whose valid window (09:59:38 to 10:54:38 on 15 Aug 2026) brackets this entire trip. Under POL-RD-23 that type (VEHICLE_BREAKDOWN) sits in the incident class the policy recognises as 'an incident', and the trip record and GPS trace show the driver voluntarily rejoined the recommended route at Paya Lebar Road / Upper Paya Lebar Road at 10:35:21 rather than extending the diversion. This is a driver managing a real-time incident on his recommended route, which is exactly the conduct POL-RD-21 protects.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-ADVIS-05005-01", "EV-CHAT-05005", "EV-TRIP-05005", "EV-GPS-05005"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-23", "POL-RD-21"],
      "relies_on": "evidence[EV-ADVIS-05005-01].payload.type",
      "asserted_weight": 0.8
    },
    {
      "text": "The contact condition of the reroute policy is satisfied on the record: the driver did use the in-app chat (message MSG-05005-003, sent 10:24:30, delivered 10:24:33, read 10:25:10), he gave a brief reason - the KPE breakdown - and the rider answered 'ok noted' (MSG-05005-004). POL-RD-21 permits notification 'by in-app chat' with 'a brief reason', and expressly provides that where the chat log confirms the rider's acknowledgment, the deviation is agreed. The rider was told of the deviation and the reason for it in the app and accepted it; he did not ask for the driver to return to the KPE or change anything while the trip was still running.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-CHAT-05005", "EV-TRIP-05005"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-21"],
      "relies_on": "payload.messages[MSG-05005-003, MSG-05005-004]",
      "asserted_weight": 0.8
    },
    {
      "text": "I concede the timing point and put it plainly: the platform's own event log places the deviation at 10:21:30, when the vehicle left the recommended route at the TPE / Upper Serangoon Road exit, and the driver's explanatory message was sent at 10:24:30 - roughly three minutes after that point. The record carries no trip call recording (trip_record.in_app_calls is empty), so no verbal notice can be established under POL-GEN-26. I do not claim the chat notice was given 'before or at the point of deviation'; it was given during the diversion, as soon as the driver informed the rider, and the rider's acknowledgment makes it an accepted reroute under POL-RD-21 even though the sequence was reason-then-acknowledgment rather than warning-then-deviation. What the judge must decide on the POL-RD-21 and POL-RD-24(a) wording is whether that late-in-time but accepted notice is the 'informed and agreed' limb; if it is, the driver keeps the fare under row (a), and if it is not, the exposure is only the excess distance fare under row (b) and is capped as claim 5 sets out.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-TRIP-05005", "EV-CHAT-05005"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-21", "POL-RD-24", "POL-GEN-26"],
      "relies_on": "trip_record.events[route_deviation_detected].at = 2026-08-15T10:21:30+08:00 vs chat messages MSG-05005-003 sent_at 10:24:30",
      "asserted_weight": 0.75
    },
    {
      "text": "Whichever way the judge reads the notice timing, the rider's money claim is bounded and cannot be 5.00 SGD. Under POL-RD-24 the only sum that can be refunded for an unjustified deviation over tolerance is the excess distance fare, and the fare model fixes it precisely: deviation 1.70 km (13.8 km actual less 12.1 km optimal, per POL-RD-22) at the base per-km rate of 0.92 SGD, with no surge, gives 1.56 SGD, and that figure is carried in the fare breakdown itself. The rider's request for 5.00 SGD therefore exceeds the policy's own computation by 3.44 SGD. If any refund issues at all it can only be 1.56 SGD, not the sum claimed, and the rider's claim for the full 5.00 SGD should be rejected on that arithmetic.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-FARE-05005", "EV-TRIP-05005"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-RD-24", "POL-RD-22"],
      "relies_on": "derived.money.excess_distance_fare_sgd = 1.56",
      "asserted_weight": 0.85
    },
    {
      "text": "This dispute is one the AI may decide rather than escalate: no safety indicator appears anywhere in the record (trip_record.cancellation_reason_code and events show an uneventful completed trip, no accident, no harassment, no feeling-unsafe allegation in either statement), neither profile carries a fraud flag (both fraud_flags are 0, so POL-GEN-22's 0.95 band is not engaged), the contested value of 5.00 SGD is below 10.00 SGD so the AI ruling is final under POL-GEN-22, and the 1.70 km deviation is within POL-RD-25's 2 km ceiling. The record is complete - trip record, 48-fix GPS trace at 100% coverage, contiguous chat log, reconciling payments ledger and the LTA advisory all carry weight 1.0 - so the judge has the facts needed to rule on the merits of the notice and the refund without a referral.",
      "evidence_ids": ["EV-TRIP-05005", "EV-GPS-05005", "EV-RPROF-05005", "EV-DPROF-05005", "EV-FARE-05005"],
      "clause_ids": ["POL-GEN-22", "POL-RD-25"],
      "relies_on": "dispute.amount_contested_sgd = 5.00; derived.deviation.deviation_km = 1.70",
      "asserted_weight": 0.8
    }
  ]
}

{
  "ruling": {
    "ruling": "PARTIAL_REFUND",
    "amount_sgd": 1.56,
    "driver_compensation_sgd": null,
    "referrals": [],
    "confidence": 0.9,
    "explanation": "This is a route-deviation dispute under the Route Deviation policy (v2.1). I have decided it on the record. Ruling: PARTIAL_REFUND — 1.56 SGD is returned to you, the rider; the rest of the charge stands; no compensation to the driver.\n\nTo the rider: I accept that the trip deviated. The platform's recorded distances are the measure under POL-RD-22 — 13.8 km actually driven against a 12.1 km recommended route — a deviation of 1.70 km, above the 1.0 km tolerance. But the only sum the policy refunds for a deviation over tolerance is the excess-distance fare, which POL-RD-24 fixes at the deviation distance times the base per-km rate without surge: 1.70 km x 0.92 SGD = 1.56 SGD (that figure is also carried in the fare breakdown, EV-FARE-05005). Your request for 5.00 SGD exceeds the policy's own computation by 3.44 SGD and cannot be granted; a refund of the whole 5.00 would require the whole trip charge or more to fall away, which no row of POL-RD-24 does.\n\nTo the driver: your reroute was traffic-justified. The LTA advisory (EV-ADVIS-05005-01) records a VEHICLE_BREAKDOWN on the Kallang-Paya Lebar Expressway — the road your recommended route uses — within the trip window, and a breakdown sits in the 'incident' class POL-RD-23 recognises. But POL-RD-21 requires you to inform the rider of a deviation by in-app chat before or at the point of deviation. The platform's own event log places the deviation at 10:21:30 (you left the recommended route at the TPE / Upper Serangoon Road exit); your explanatory message (MSG-05005-003) was sent at 10:24:30, about three minutes later. That is a breach of the timing requirement, and your statement that you informed the passenger 'before' you changed route is not borne out by the record. The rider's 'ok noted' (MSG-05005-004) is an acknowledgment of a reroute already underway; it does not make a late notice the notice the clause requires, because POL-RD-21's 'agreed' provision presupposes a notice given before or at the point of deviation. Row (a) of POL-RD-24 — traffic-justified, rider informed and agreed — is therefore not available, and the excess-distance fare is refunded under row (b). I note that if the advisory were instead read as not intersecting your route, row (d) would give exactly the same 1.56 SGD, so that question does not change the outcome.\n\nWhy the AI can rule rather than hold: the contested amount is 5.00 SGD, below POL-GEN-22's 10.00 SGD band, so the ruling is final; the deviation is 1.70 km, within POL-RD-25's 2 km ceiling; there is no safety indicator in the record (POL-GEN-21) and no fraud flag on either profile (POL-GEN-22). The fact POL-GEN-26 protects — whether you were notified — is established on the chat log and the trip event log, both weight 1.0.\n\nWhat happens next: 1.56 SGD of the 23.04 SGD charged is returned to the rider. No referral issues (the Route Deviation policy carries no referral clause), and the driver receives no compensation."
  },
  "discarded_claims": []
}

