    "explanation": "To both parties: the recommended route was 12.1 km and the route actually driven was 13.8 km, a deviation of 1.70 km (POL-RD-22, read from the platform's recorded distances). That is over the 1.0 km tolerance and within the 2 km ceiling at which the AI may rule (POL-RD-25). The trip record and GPS trace show the vehicle left the recommended route at the TPE / Upper Serangoon Road exit at 10:21:30 and rejoined at Paya Lebar Road at 10:35:21. POL-RD-21 requires the driver to inform the rider by in-app chat before or at the point of deviation. The driver's only message on the reroute, MSG-05005-003, was sent at 10:24:30 — about three minutes after the vehicle had already turned off — and the record carries no call recording, so verbal notice cannot be established (POL-GEN-26). The driver's argument that the chat satisfies POL-RD-21 and makes this an agreed reroute under row (a) of POL-RD-24 is not accepted: the notice was not given before or at the point of deviation, and the rider's 'ok noted' at 10:25:00 acknowledges a reroute already under way rather than agreeing to it in advance. Whether or not the KPE breakdown advisory (EV-ADVIS-05005-01) makes the reroute traffic-justified, the outcome is the same — under row (b) or row (d) of POL-RD-24 the rider is refunded the excess distance fare. That is 1.70 km × 0.92 SGD/km = 1.56 SGD, the figure the fare breakdown itself carries. The rider's request for 5.00 SGD is 3.44 SGD above what the policy provides and is not payable. The rider was charged 23.04 SGD with no refund issued, so 1.56 SGD is returned to the rider as part of the trip's charge. Confidence is 0.90, above the 0.80 POL-RD-25 requires; the contested 5.00 SGD is below POL-GEN-22's 10.00 SGD band, so this ruling is final; no safety flag and no fraud flag apply. Route deviation carries no referral clause in this version, so none is made."
